--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracking ticket 0252 (model C, ratified 2026-07-13). Incident I2
(aedist, 2026-06-11/12): a raid session checked out its branch in the
ORCHESTRATOR's worktree path — generic names (`t{N}`) mean two sessions
working the same ticket resolve to the same worktree path. A session
discriminator in the name makes sibling entry detectable and collisions
structurally rare. Audit: docs/audit-0252-worktree-ownership-2026-07-12.md.

Blocked-by 0294 (child of 0251): it generalizes hunt step 3's
worktree-ownership predicate (t<id> vs agent-<id>); this naming change must
land on top of that predicate, not race it.

## Relevant files

- `rules/workflow.md` — the worktree naming table
- `skills/hunt/SKILL.md`, `skills/raid/SKILL.md` — worktree creation sites
- `scripts/beat.py` — `_HARNESS_WORKTREE_RE` must accept the new suffix

## Actions

1. Extend the naming convention to `t{N}-{discriminator}` (short session
   PID or equivalent short unique token) in the rules/workflow.md table.
2. Update hunt/raid worktree-creation instructions to apply the suffix.
3. Update `_HARNESS_WORKTREE_RE` in beat.py (and any other name-pattern
   consumers found by grep) to accept both old and new forms during the
   transition.

## Test

- beat.py regex unit test: matches `t0296-1234a`, still matches legacy `t0296`.

## Verification

- [ ] Two simulated sessions on the same ticket produce distinct worktree paths
- [ ] beat.py cleanup still recognizes harness worktrees under the new names
- [ ] Grep sweep confirms no other consumer parses worktree names

## Invariants

- Legacy `t{N}` worktrees remain recognized until they cycle out

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Naming table, hunt/raid, and beat.py updated coherently
- [ ] `make check` passes
